This program first constructs a graph from the input roads to identify connected components. It then uses depth-first search (DFS) to find the shortest route modulo L in different possible ways of reaching destination order (by swapping which group A or B reaches final destination). It retrieves nodes by traversing a node queue and stores nodes in a string separated by spaces. By checking the modular length against L to avoid overlap gaps, the min function calculates the modular shortest L-way cycle route. If a route stops at exactly L values, it updates the temporary shortest L-way modulo. Also, output next allows printing the minimum route length with Bob and Alex's paths.

